1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:01,867
Picture two Moons tonight.

2
00:00:01,867 --> 00:00:02,957
Ours, and a twin.

3
00:00:02,957 --> 00:00:04,496
Here's the strange part.

4
00:00:04,496 --> 00:00:07,189
Two Moons would not give you bigger tides.

5
00:00:07,189 --> 00:00:11,613
Park the twin in the right spot, and the tides stay exactly the same.

6
00:00:12,313 --> 00:00:18,628
But park it in the wrong spot, and it rips itself apart, or drowns our coasts, or simply flies away.

7
00:00:18,628 --> 00:00:23,428
Today we try five parking spots, and find out which ones keep a second Moon.

8
00:00:24,128 --> 00:00:24,738
The rules.

9
00:00:24,738 --> 00:00:27,731
Our new Moon is an exact twin of the one we have.

10
00:00:27,731 --> 00:00:32,677
Three thousand four hundred and seventy four kilometers wide, with the same mass.

11
00:00:32,677 --> 00:00:35,242
The only thing we choose is where it goes.

12
00:00:35,942 --> 00:00:37,306
Here's the parking lot.

13
00:00:37,306 --> 00:00:43,888
Earth at the left, our Moon in the middle, and the edge of Earth's gravity a million and a half kilometers out.

14
00:00:43,888 --> 00:00:46,319
The scale is squeezed so everything fits.

15
00:00:46,319 --> 00:00:46,971
Five spots.

16
00:00:46,971 --> 00:00:48,038
Let's start close.

17
00:00:48,738 --> 00:00:49,313
Spot one.

18
00:00:49,313 --> 00:00:52,381
Fifteen thousand kilometers from Earth's center.

19
00:00:52,381 --> 00:00:56,216
That's only about nine thousand kilometers above the ground.

20
00:00:56,216 --> 00:00:59,092
Closer than the satellites that bring you TV.

21
00:00:59,092 --> 00:01:04,653
For comparison, the International Space Station flies about four hundred kilometers up.

22
00:01:05,353 --> 00:01:07,029
It doesn't survive the trip.

23
00:01:07,029 --> 00:01:11,760
Earth's gravity pulls harder on the near side of the Moon than on the far side.

24
00:01:11,760 --> 00:01:15,592
Inside about eighteen thousand kilometers, that difference wins.

25
00:01:15,592 --> 00:01:19,305
It's called the Roche limit, and the twin is torn into pieces.

26
00:01:20,005 --> 00:01:24,912
The pieces spread around the planet, and Earth ends up with rings like Saturn.

27
00:01:24,912 --> 00:01:27,365
Beautiful, but it's not a Moon anymore.

28
00:01:27,365 --> 00:01:28,624
Verdict: torn apart.

29
00:01:29,324 --> 00:01:29,913
Spot two.

30
00:01:29,913 --> 00:01:31,683
Thirty thousand kilometers.

31
00:01:31,683 --> 00:01:33,584
Just outside the danger line.

32
00:01:33,584 --> 00:01:35,025
And the sky goes wild.

33
00:01:35,025 --> 00:01:39,089
The twin would look almost thirteen times wider than our Moon.

34
00:01:39,089 --> 00:01:41,645
Hold three fingers out at arm's length.

35
00:01:41,645 --> 00:01:42,956
It's about that big.

36
00:01:43,656 --> 00:01:45,381
And it moves the wrong way.

37
00:01:45,381 --> 00:01:50,174
It circles Earth in just over fourteen hours, faster than the planet turns.

38
00:01:50,174 --> 00:01:54,967
So it rises in the west and sets in the east, roughly every day and a half.

39
00:01:54,967 --> 00:01:57,459
Mars has a moon that does exactly this.

40
00:01:57,459 --> 00:01:57,906
Phobos.

41
00:01:58,606 --> 00:02:00,646
The tides are the real problem.

42
00:02:00,646 --> 00:02:03,542
Tidal pull grows fast as a moon gets closer.

43
00:02:03,542 --> 00:02:08,676
Cut the distance by thirteen, and the pull jumps more than two thousand times.

44
00:02:09,376 --> 00:02:10,531
And it can't stay.

45
00:02:10,531 --> 00:02:18,493
Below about forty two thousand kilometers, a moon orbits faster than its planet spins, and the tides it raises drag it down.

46
00:02:18,493 --> 00:02:23,180
Phobos is falling toward Mars about one point eight meters every century.

47
00:02:23,180 --> 00:02:28,316
Our twin would spiral in the same way, until it reached spot one, and shattered.

48
00:02:28,316 --> 00:02:29,343
Verdict: doomed.

49
00:02:30,044 --> 00:02:30,752
Spot three.

50
00:02:30,752 --> 00:02:32,814
One hundred thousand kilometers.

51
00:02:32,814 --> 00:02:38,998
About a quarter of the way to our Moon, and safely above that forty two thousand kilometer line.

52
00:02:38,998 --> 00:02:43,057
Here, it drifts outward instead, just like our Moon does today.

53
00:02:43,757 --> 00:02:45,180
Now the sky is gorgeous.

54
00:02:45,180 --> 00:02:48,855
Two Moons, one of them almost four times wider than the other.

55
00:02:48,855 --> 00:02:54,605
At full phase, the big one would light the night about fifteen times brighter than our full Moon.

56
00:02:54,605 --> 00:02:56,384
You could read a book outside.

57
00:02:56,384 --> 00:03:03,853
And since it moves the same way Earth turns, it would crawl across the sky, taking about sixteen hours from rising to setting.

58
00:03:04,553 --> 00:03:07,339
It's nearly four times wider than the Sun, too.

59
00:03:07,339 --> 00:03:12,615
Whenever it passed in front of the Sun, it would cover it completely, with room to spare.

60
00:03:12,615 --> 00:03:16,884
Solar eclipses would last much longer than the few minutes we get today.

61
00:03:17,584 --> 00:03:18,428
And the tides.

62
00:03:18,428 --> 00:03:22,530
The twin pulls on the oceans fifty seven times harder than our Moon.

63
00:03:22,530 --> 00:03:28,020
And because it orbits in three and a half days, its tides fall out of step with the Moon's.

64
00:03:28,020 --> 00:03:29,166
Look at the harbor.

65
00:03:29,166 --> 00:03:31,037
Some days the two pulls add up.

66
00:03:31,037 --> 00:03:32,303
Some days they fight.

67
00:03:33,003 --> 00:03:34,374
There's a hidden cost.

68
00:03:34,374 --> 00:03:36,804
Tides act like a brake on Earth's spin.

69
00:03:36,804 --> 00:03:41,352
Today the Moon stretches our day by about two milliseconds every century.

70
00:03:41,352 --> 00:03:49,140
Our rough estimate, using today's tidal friction: the twin at spot three would brake Earth about three thousand times harder.

71
00:03:49,140 --> 00:03:51,009
Several seconds every century.

72
00:03:51,709 --> 00:03:55,187
Over millions of years, our days would grow long, fast.

73
00:03:55,187 --> 00:03:56,958
But the twin itself is safe.

74
00:03:56,958 --> 00:03:59,487
It slowly drifts away and stays with us.

75
00:03:59,487 --> 00:04:02,333
Verdict: it stays, and it changes everything.

76
00:04:03,033 --> 00:04:04,876
Spot four is the clever one.

77
00:04:04,876 --> 00:04:09,419
Same distance as our Moon, same orbit, but sixty degrees ahead of it.

78
00:04:09,419 --> 00:04:12,973
Earth, the Moon, and the twin form a perfect triangle.

79
00:04:12,973 --> 00:04:16,067
This is a special balance point, called L four.

80
00:04:16,767 --> 00:04:17,518
Is it stable?

81
00:04:17,518 --> 00:04:20,749
There's a test from eighteen seventy five, Routh's rule.

82
00:04:20,749 --> 00:04:22,077
It compares the masses.

83
00:04:22,077 --> 00:04:25,943
Earth is eighty one times heavier than the Moon, and that's enough.

84
00:04:25,943 --> 00:04:29,694
Our calculation says even a full sized twin would hold its place.

85
00:04:29,694 --> 00:04:33,041
It would only rock gently back and forth around the point.

86
00:04:33,041 --> 00:04:39,850
Jupiter already does this with more than ten thousand asteroids, the Trojans, sixty degrees ahead of it and behind it.

87
00:04:40,550 --> 00:04:42,779
And here is the hook we promised.

88
00:04:42,779 --> 00:04:48,657
With a twin sixty degrees ahead, the two tides overlap in a way that cancels the extra.

89
00:04:48,657 --> 00:04:53,048
Our calculation: the high tide is exactly as tall as it is today.

90
00:04:53,048 --> 00:04:54,872
Just about two hours later.

91
00:04:54,872 --> 00:04:56,358
Two Moons, same tides.

92
00:04:57,058 --> 00:04:58,645
The sky would be odd, too.

93
00:04:58,645 --> 00:05:01,454
The two would never be full on the same night.

94
00:05:01,454 --> 00:05:06,765
When ours is full, the twin is only three quarters lit, a few days ahead in its phases.

95
00:05:07,465 --> 00:05:08,356
Now the twist.

96
00:05:08,356 --> 00:05:10,265
This may have really happened.

97
00:05:10,265 --> 00:05:22,931
In twenty eleven, two scientists, Martin Jutzi and Erik Asphaug, suggested that the early Earth had a second, smaller moon, about twelve hundred kilometers wide, parked at exactly this kind of point.

98
00:05:23,631 --> 00:05:29,312
As the Moon drifted away from Earth, the balance broke, and the companion slowly caught up.

99
00:05:29,312 --> 00:05:32,995
It hit the Moon at only two to three kilometers per second.

100
00:05:32,995 --> 00:05:35,117
Slow enough not to blast a crater.

101
00:05:35,117 --> 00:05:37,989
It splattered across one side, like a pancake.

102
00:05:38,689 --> 00:05:40,993
And that might explain a real mystery.

103
00:05:40,993 --> 00:05:49,843
The far side of the Moon, the side we never see, is covered in thick highlands, very different from the side facing us, with a much thicker crust.

104
00:05:49,843 --> 00:05:52,632
Maybe that's the scar of our lost second Moon.

105
00:05:52,632 --> 00:05:54,390
Verdict: stable, for a while.

106
00:05:55,090 --> 00:05:55,692
Spot five.

107
00:05:55,692 --> 00:05:58,459
A million kilometers out, far beyond our Moon.

108
00:05:58,459 --> 00:06:06,640
Here the twin would take a hundred and fourteen days to go around once, and it would look smaller than our Moon, less than half as wide.

109
00:06:06,640 --> 00:06:10,490
Its tides would be about seventeen times weaker than the Moon's.

110
00:06:10,490 --> 00:06:11,453
Barely a ripple.

111
00:06:12,153 --> 00:06:15,115
But out here, Earth isn't the only one pulling.

112
00:06:15,115 --> 00:06:16,250
The Sun pulls too.

113
00:06:16,250 --> 00:06:26,019
Earth's gravity rules out to about one and a half million kilometers, but orbits that go the same way as the Moon only stay safe inside about half of that.

114
00:06:26,719 --> 00:06:34,171
So the Sun tugs, and the twin's path gets wilder, until it slips out of Earth's grip and goes around the Sun on its own.

115
00:06:34,171 --> 00:06:37,587
In our simple simulation, that takes about five months.

116
00:06:37,587 --> 00:06:39,264
Verdict: stolen by the Sun.

117
00:06:39,963 --> 00:06:41,462
So only a few spots work.

118
00:06:41,462 --> 00:06:43,201
But here's the last surprise.

119
00:06:43,201 --> 00:06:45,600
Earth catches second Moons all the time.

120
00:06:45,600 --> 00:06:46,679
They're just tiny.

121
00:06:46,679 --> 00:06:53,994
In two thousand six, a rock about three meters wide was caught and looped around Earth for about a year before it escaped.

122
00:06:53,994 --> 00:07:02,747
Astronomers estimate that at almost any moment, at least one rock about a meter wide is looping around Earth, too small and dark for us to notice.

123
00:07:03,447 --> 00:07:09,486
Another one, barely a meter wide, was spotted in twenty twenty, after years of looping around us.

124
00:07:09,486 --> 00:07:14,280
And in late twenty twenty four, a ten meter rock tagged along for two months.

125
00:07:14,280 --> 00:07:15,961
Its colors match Moon rock.

126
00:07:15,961 --> 00:07:19,447
It may be a piece of our own Moon, blasted off long ago.

127
00:07:20,147 --> 00:07:21,465
And there's one more.

128
00:07:21,465 --> 00:07:27,237
Kamo'oalewa doesn't orbit Earth, but it circles the Sun right alongside us, year after year.

129
00:07:27,237 --> 00:07:28,053
A quasi moon.

130
00:07:28,053 --> 00:07:32,884
This year, China's Tianwen two probe reached it, to bring a piece of it home.

131
00:07:33,583 --> 00:07:35,441
So, our five parking spots.

132
00:07:35,441 --> 00:07:38,194
Fifteen thousand kilometers, torn apart.

133
00:07:38,194 --> 00:07:40,327
Thirty thousand, it spirals in.

134
00:07:40,327 --> 00:07:43,836
A hundred thousand, it stays and floods the coasts.

135
00:07:43,836 --> 00:07:47,620
In the Moon's own orbit, same tides, and maybe history.

136
00:07:47,620 --> 00:07:50,373
A million kilometers, stolen by the Sun.

137
00:07:51,073 --> 00:07:56,956
Next time, we shrink the whole universe until the Sun is a grain of sand, and then we walk to the edge.

138
00:07:56,956 --> 00:07:59,641
Subscribe to Cosmic Scale so you don't miss it.

139
00:07:59,641 --> 00:08:03,297
And tell me in the comments, where would you park a second Moon?
